---
registryId: dedoc-systems
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Dedoc?

Dedoc is an open-source system that converts documents from different formats into a
unified structured representation. It can extract textual lines in reading order,
format annotations, hierarchy, tables, attachments, and metadata. It can run as a
Python library, an API service, or a container.

It matters because downstream search, review, analytics, and retrieval systems need
more than a blob of text. They need to know which text is a heading, which cells form a
table, which attachment belongs to the source, and how every output fragment can be
traced back to the input.

{% callout variant="info" %}
The parser route must match the document's actual representation and the downstream
output contract. OCR, table analysis, attachment recursion, and
hierarchy extraction are explicit quality and capacity decisions, not universal
defaults.
{% /callout %}

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Content" eyebrow="Reading order" icon="document" tone="blue" %}
Textual lines and annotations preserve what was read and enough formatting context to
render or inspect it.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Structure" eyebrow="Hierarchy" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Line types and hierarchy levels are constructed into a tree or a linear output,
depending on the requested representation.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Objects" eyebrow="Beyond text" icon="table" tone="green" %}
Tables, attachments, and metadata remain separate objects so downstream code does not
have to reconstruct them from flattened prose.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Follow the documented parsing pipeline

Dedoc separates format handling from structure construction. That separation is useful
in production because a conversion failure, reader failure, structure error, and output
contract violation need different retries and ownership.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Convert when needed" label="Converter" %}
Normalize formats that can be converted to a supported representation, such as older
office documents to their newer container formats.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Read the document" label="Reader" %}
Produce an intermediate document with lines, annotations, tables, attachments, and
metadata. PDF routing may use native text, image recognition, or page-level detection.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Extract structure" label="Classifier and rules" %}
Assign line types and hierarchy levels using a domain-specific extractor or the default
structure path.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Construct output" label="Contract" %}
Build a tree or linear representation, serialize it in the requested format, and retain
source lineage for review.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

The [official workflow documentation](https://dedoc.readthedocs.io/en/stable/)
describes converters, readers, structure extractors, and structure constructors as
separate stages.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Size the worker fleet from page work, not file count

Two documents of equal byte size can have very different service times. A born-digital
PDF can reuse its text layer, while a scan may require page rendering, orientation,
recognition, and table analysis. Averages hide the malformed and OCR-heavy tail that
sets queue age and memory pressure.

The capacity lab exposes five independent demand drivers:

- documents arriving in the target processing window;
- pages per document;
- the share of pages that require OCR;
- the share requiring table analysis;
- concurrent workers and their per-process memory envelope.

{% interactive-block id="ml-systems/dedoc-systems-calculator" /%}

Use the result to design a benchmark matrix, not as a throughput guarantee. Measure
native PDF, scanned PDF, mixed-page PDF, office files, tables, attachments, malformed
inputs, and the largest allowed document separately. Track p50, p95, and p99 service
time as well as peak resident memory.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Route each format to the correct reader and parameters

Reader selection changes both quality and cost. Dedoc's PDF parameters distinguish
native textual layers from image-only pages, support automatic or page-level detection,
and can enable expensive table analysis only when the output contract requires it.

{% interactive-block id="ml-systems/dedoc-systems-routing-lab" /%}

### Important routing decisions

- Use `pdf_with_text_layer=auto_tabby` when the system should detect the PDF path and
  reuse a trustworthy text layer when present.
- Enable `each_page_textual_layer_detection=true` for mixed binders where the first
  pages do not represent the whole document.
- Force the image path only when the input contract says the PDF is scanned or the
  native encoding is unusable; OCR adds latency and recognition error.
- Disable table analysis only when losing row and cell relationships is acceptable.
- Use native office readers when they can preserve paragraphs, tables, annotations,
  metadata, and attachments without rendering pages.

The current [API parameter reference](https://dedoc.readthedocs.io/en/stable/dedoc_api_usage/api.html)
documents PDF text-layer detection, table analysis, page slices, orientation,
attachments, structure types, and return formats.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Define the parsed output as a versioned contract

A parser returning HTTP 200 does not prove the output is usable. Validate the fields
the downstream consumer needs and attach quality evidence to each parsed artifact.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Coverage" value="Text / pages / objects" detail="What proportion of expected content was recovered?" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric label="Structure" value="Types / levels / order" detail="Do headings, lists, and reading order preserve the document meaning?" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Tables" value="Rows / cells / spans" detail="Are table relationships intact enough for the consumer?" tone="green" /%}
{% metric label="Lineage" value="Source / page / route" detail="Can a reviewer trace every important output to its input and parser configuration?" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Route from an inspected input contract

{% code-block file="/api/content/ml-systems/dedoc-systems/code/route_document.py" language="python" title="Choose explicit Dedoc parameters" /%}

The routing function keeps policy outside the HTTP client. In a real service, detect
the media type from content, constrain the allowed formats, and record the selected
reader and parameters with the result.

### Validate before publishing downstream

{% code-block file="/api/content/ml-systems/dedoc-systems/code/validate_output_contract.py" language="python" title="Check the required parsed-output shape" /%}

Expand the validator with page coverage, table expectations, attachment inventory,
confidence thresholds, and a schema version. Send uncertain documents to review or a
fallback parser instead of silently publishing partial structure.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Wrap the parser in a bounded production service

Dedoc supplies parsing behavior; the surrounding system must supply isolation,
admission control, lifecycle state, storage, retries, and observability.

{% system-flow title="Production document processing path" caption="Keep untrusted bytes, parser work, validation, and published output in separate operational stages." %}
{% system-node title="Quarantine input" eyebrow="Ingress" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Verify media type, size, page and attachment limits, malware policy, tenant identity,
and an idempotency key before parsing.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Route and enqueue" eyebrow="Control plane" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
Inspect the document profile, select parameters, assign a deadline and retry budget,
then place the job on a bounded queue.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Parse in isolation" eyebrow="Worker" icon="processor" tone="violet" %}
Run converters, native readers, OCR, and table analysis with CPU, memory, filesystem,
network, and execution-time limits.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Validate and publish" eyebrow="Output gate" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Check coverage and schema, store immutable input and output references, then publish a
versioned result or a review task.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Model job state explicitly

1. `accepted`: input passed admission checks and has an immutable source reference;
2. `queued`: a route, deadline, and retry budget are recorded;
3. `running`: one worker lease owns the attempt;
4. `review_required`: output exists but misses a quality or confidence gate;
5. `succeeded`: the versioned output contract is published exactly once;
6. `failed`: the final reason is classified as input, conversion, reader, resource, or
   contract failure.

Retries should create new attempts under the same job identity. Publishing must be
idempotent because a worker can finish after its lease or client connection is lost.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Secure and operate untrusted document processing

### Contain the input

- cap bytes, pages, archive depth, attachment count, extracted size, and total work;
- isolate converters and OCR processes with no ambient cloud credentials;
- disable unnecessary network access and use short-lived, least-privilege object URLs;
- reject path traversal, archive expansion, unsupported media, and recursive attachment
  patterns before they consume the fleet;
- encrypt source and result objects and apply tenant-scoped retention and deletion.

### Observe the contract

- queue depth and oldest-job age by tenant, format, and route;
- parser latency, timeout, retry, memory, and worker-recycle rates;
- native-versus-OCR routing, page-level fallback, and table-analysis share;
- coverage, structure, table, and review rates by document population;
- failures by converter, reader, parameter set, and release version.

### Release deliberately

- maintain a versioned golden corpus with native, scanned, mixed, rotated, table-heavy,
  attachment-heavy, malformed, and adversarial files;
- compare structural output, not only extracted character count;
- canary parser, OCR, model, and parameter changes by document population;
- keep the prior route and output schema available until downstream consumers verify
  compatibility;
- replay stored inputs into a shadow pipeline before changing the default route.
{% /section-card %}

